<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Vermont Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1777 CE – 1791 CE · capital Windsor</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An unrecognised republic in the Green Mountains that governed itself for fourteen years, with the first constitution to ban adult slavery, before joining the United States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> were claimed by both the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-new-hampshire">Province of New Hampshire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-new-york">Province of New York</a>. Settlers holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> grants resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethan-allen">Ethan Allen's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-mountain-boys">Green Mountain Boys</a>, and in 1777 conventions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-vermont">Westminster</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windsor-vermont">Windsor</a> declared an independent state, first called New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and then Vermont.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The 1777 constitution prohibited adult <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, gave the vote to men without property qualifications and set up public schools. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-chittenden">Thomas Chittenden</a> served as governor almost throughout. Vermont coined its own money, ran a postal service and naturalised citizens. Congress refused admission while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> objected, and leaders including the Allens held secret talks with British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> in the early 1780s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> gave up its claim in 1790 for a payment of $30,000, Vermont was admitted in 1791 as the fourteenth state, the first after the original thirteen. Its constitution became a model for later state charters.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vermont-republic.jpg" alt="Vermont Republic" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: Pubhouseburly · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vermont_Republic_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1777 CE – 1791 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Windsor</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas Chittenden, Moses Robinson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Congregationalism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Vermont, State of Vermont (1777-1791)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=vermont-republic" aria-label="Vermont Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2938 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vermont Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3258 892L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3258 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3258 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Windsor. <a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=vermont-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
